\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Benchmark scores drive model selection and research priorities for large language models, yet training data contamination is pervasive: trillion-token web crawls routinely ingest benchmark items \citep{oren2024proving}, and practitioners cannot determine how much of a reported score reflects genuine capability versus memorization \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu,chang2025benchmarking}. Despite twenty mitigation strategies \citep{sun2025emperor}, contamination-free benchmarks \citep{white2024livebench}, and dynamic regeneration \citep{dong_2024_cdd}, a fundamental question remains: what is the actual effect of contamination on aggregate scores? The implicit assumption is uniform inflation, but this has never been tested across model families.

We perform controlled SFT injection (LoRA, five dosage levels) into six models spanning five independent families from five labs (Alibaba, Microsoft, Allen AI, Google, TII) and discover a spectrum of dose-response patterns (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response})---from immunity (Phi-4, $\pm$0.5pp) and benefit ($+$3--4pp, three families) to collapse (Qwen-14B, $-$9 to $-$25pp across seeds).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{MMLU accuracy vs.\ contamination dosage (5-shot, seed$=$42; LLaMA-3.1 at seed$=$0 with d000/d025/d100). Immune and Benefit replicate across families; Collapse and V-recovery are Qwen2.5-specific. Cross-seed patterns in Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}; ARC in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

Across six models, three lower-baseline models benefit ($+$3.2 to $+$4.2pp), one is immune (Phi-4), and the remaining two (both Qwen2.5) show harm---with the 14B model 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B. These effects are benchmark-conditional: the same model can collapse on MMLU ($-$9.4pp) yet gain on GSM8K ($+$13pp). ContamCal, our diagnostic framework combining multi-signal exposure mapping with contamination-augmented IRT (2PL+$\gamma_k$), enables this characterization.

Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dose-response profiles with cross-seed and cross-benchmark validation.} Controlled injection across five families reveals two replicated patterns (Immune, Benefit) and two preliminary Qwen2.5-specific patterns (Collapse, V-recovery). Out-of-sample validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms generalizability. Cross-benchmark transfer reveals three family-specific patterns on GSM8K (null, consistent, directional reversal). Multi-seed evaluation ($n\!=\!3$) shows direction is reproducible but magnitude is seed-sensitive. Domain analysis reveals within-benchmark heterogeneity (Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$).
    \item \textbf{No detectable item-specific memorization at current power levels.} Difference-in-differences analysis on 14{,}042 MMLU items finds no differential accuracy gain for contaminated vs.\ clean items in any model family (MDES 1.2--2.0pp on MMLU; ARC power is limited at 2.3--5.6pp). Results are consistent with global capability modulation, challenging the assumption that contamination operates primarily through item-specific memorization.
    \item \textbf{Diagnostic pipeline requirements and signal failure.} Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits directional reversal at 0.5B (paired Cohen's $d = -1.98$ at d100; $|d| > 1.4$ across benchmarks at d005). RRF-based exposure scoring improves IRT rank correlation ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random exposure). Simulation establishes $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ at AUC $\geq 0.55$, above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$).
\end{enumerate}

